## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of practical, beginner-friendly schema evolution across HTTP input, DB writes, and Kafka events without breaking existing consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Backward-compatible schema evolution
- Optional/additive fields and defaults
- Schema registry with compatibility levels
- Canary rollout and feature flags
- Contract and end-to-end testing

## Code Example

```javascript
const Ajv = require("ajv");
const ajv = new Ajv();
const schema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    id: { type: "string" },
    event: { type: "string" },
    customer_segment: { type: "string" }
  },
  required: ["id", "event"],
  additionalProperties: true
};
function validate(payload){
  const valid = ajv.validate(schema, payload);
  if(!valid) throw new Error(ajv.errors.map(e=>e.message).join(", "));
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle a future breaking change if a client needs the new field immediately?
- How would you monitor and alert on schema compatibility errors in prod?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design practical backpressure and degradation strategies for a CNPA stack under load, balancing user impact with data integrity.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and load shedding
- Degraded paths and asynchronous processing
- Idempotency and deduplication across HTTP, DB, and Kafka
- Observability: latency, error rate, queue depth, saturation signals

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.burst = burst; // max tokens
    this.tokens = burst;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  consume(n = 1) {
    this._refresh();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
  _refresh() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement end-to-end idempotency to prevent duplicates during backpressure? 
- What metrics and rollback criteria would you rely on to trigger a full rollback or canary pause?

## Why This Is Asked
Advanced multi-region CNPA scenarios demand precise fault-localization across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and cross-region replication. This question probes systematic debugging, observability design, and practical fixes that preserve data integrity and latency.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-region Kafka replication and lag diagnosis
- End-to-end tracing across services
- Producer batching, acks, and idempotent writes
